course: dictionary
lesson: 15695
title: Toddle
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A toddler is a small child who has just learned to walk. When a toddler walks, the steps are short and wobbly, and that is called toddling. «The baby held the sofa and toddled across the room.» The steps are short and wobbly, so toddled means walked with small, unsteady steps.
explain: Context helps again. «Mum said, "I'll toddle off to the shop now."» Mum is not a baby, and she is not wobbling, so toddle must mean something else here. Ask: what would fit? Try "go" in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Toddle is often used in a friendly, relaxed way, as in «toddle along» or «toddle over to». These phrases mean going somewhere calmly and slowly. The clues are the easy, unhurried tone of the sentence.
fact: To toddle means to walk with short, wobbly steps, like a small child learning to walk.
fact: A toddler is a small child who has just learned to walk.
fact: Informally, to toddle somewhere means to walk or go there slowly and calmly.
fact: «I'll toddle off now» is a friendly way of saying «I'll go».
fact: Useful phrases: «toddle along», «toddle off», «toddle over to».
exercise: «The baby toddled across the kitchen floor, holding on to the chair.» What does toddled mean here? | Crawled on the floor | * Walked with short, wobbly steps | Fell asleep | Ran very fast
exercise: Dad says, «I'll toddle over to the shop, and I'll be back soon.» What does toddle mean here? | * Go calmly and slowly | Jump high in the air | Shout loudly
exercise: «The toddler clapped her hands when she took her first steps.» Find and copy the word that names a small child who is learning to walk. | * toddler | clapped | hands | laughed
test: «Grandma said, "Let's toddle along to the park."» What does toddle along mean? | Swim across the lake | Hop on one foot | * Walk along in an easy, unhurried way | Stop and sit down
test: Which sentence uses toddle in the friendly, informal way? | * "I'll toddle off now," said Aunt Ruth, grabbing her coat. | Tom fell over while he toddled, and his mum scooped him up. | The toddler clapped as he took his first steps.
exam: The little boy ___ across the playground, one wobbly step at a time. | Galloped | Slid | * Toddled | Sprinted
